\f1\fs22 Pastor Rex Humble's leather-soled shoes squeaked across the polished floor of what had once been the Memphis Mafia Bar, and was now the Memphis Mafia Bar & House of Worship\'97an unholy amalgamation that reflected the schizophrenic nature of his flock. He'd come to retrieve the sacramental water (actually just regular water in recycled bourbon bottles) when he noticed something peculiar: a line of kitchen staff fidgeting outside the walk-in cooler.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"What's the meaning of this congregation?" he demanded, tugging at his white jumpsuit with its embroidered cross-guitar emblem.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A nervous Elvi server averted his eyes. "Waiting our turn, Pastor. For the... blessing."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Blessing?" Humble's pompadour seemed to quiver with suspicion. "What blessing requires refrigeration?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Before anyone could answer, a sound emerged from the cooler\'97a rhythmic thumping accompanied by muffled exclamations that were decidedly not prayers, at least not of any denomination Humble recognized.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He yanked open the heavy steel door to reveal a tableau that would have given Hieronymus Bosch pause: a dozen Elvi and Elvines engaged in activities that could only generously be described as \'93communion.\'94 They writhed between hanging sides of beef, knocking containers of spices and marinades from shelves. A pair of particularly enthusiastic worshippers had transformed a prep table into an altar of an entirely different religion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"BLASPHEMY!" Humble roared, his voice cracking like lightning across the frigid air.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The copulating Elvi froze, their sequinned jumpsuits and leather pants in various states of disarray.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Pastor Rex!" gasped a female Evolution Elvi known as Memphis Marie. "We're just spreading the immunity, as the High Elvis decreed."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Immunity?" Humble sputtered, reaching instinctively for something to smite with. His hand found a massive side of premium pork ribs, which he swung with righteous fury. It connected with the nearest male Elvi's head, dropping him into a container of coleslaw.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"This... this orgiastic abomination is how you fight Dunhilton's plague?" Humble demanded, the terrible truth dawning on him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The Humpening," another Elvi confirmed proudly. "The Great Teacher's blessing protects us from the virus. We're just passing it along, like evolutionary missionaries."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Humble staggered backward as if struck. "The very concept offends the sanctity of our gospel existence!" He raised his arms dramatically. "Hear me, servants of the true faith! No Gospel Elvi shall partake in this... this unholy Humpening! We shall remain pure and let faith shield us from earthly plagues!"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Within hours, his proclamation spread throughout the Quarter. The Gospel Elvi stood firm in their rejection of the Evolution Elvi's immunity-spreading techniques, proudly singing hymns while their counterparts engaged in what Humble described as "activities that would make even Las Vegas Elvis blush."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The results manifested within days. Gospel Elvi began falling ill, their numbers dwindling with alarming speed. Presented with irrefutable evidence that the Humpening offered protection, they faced a stark choice: abandon their gospel principles or perish.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Many converted, sheepishly joining the Evolution ranks and their immunity-conferring rituals. Others, led by Humble himself, doubled down on their faith, insisting that spiritual purity would eventually prevail over bodily weakness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Pastor Rex Humble was among the last to succumb, his fever-wracked body propped up in his makeshift pulpit. As consciousness faded, he gazed up at the neon-lit ceiling of his once-thriving Gospel House, now empty save for a few equally doomed acolytes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The Lord works in mysterious ways," he whispered through cracked lips. His final thought, as darkness closed in, was that the Almighty's methods had lately become not so much mysterious as incomprehensible, as if the universe itself had decided to rewrite its own operating manual with a sense of humour that bordered on the cosmic.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In his last moments, he couldn't help but wonder if perhaps Darwin had been onto something after all.
